WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.100
At each of Whole Foods Market's

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.540
more than 500 American stores,

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.440
managers ask every team member,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.630
from the meat clerks to the baristas

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:11.480
to the janitorial staff, to orient their work

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.360
around a shared purpose, which is to make natural

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.590
and healthy food widely available. This goal, according

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:20.630
to Whole Foods CEO and founder John Mackey,

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:25.010
is in no way inconsistent with maximizing shareholder value.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:26.777
As Mackey writes in his new book,

00:00:26.777 --> 00:00:28.830
"At the heart of conscious capitalism

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.040
is a radical refutation of the negative perceptions

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.410
of business, and a rejection of the split

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:36.200
between purpose and profit."

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.190
At a Soho Forum debate held on February 18th, 2020,

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.890
Ayn Rand Institute Chair Yaron Brook challenged this view.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.090
He believes that maximizing profit should always be the

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:50.070
primary goal of companies, and it's that focus, which

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:53.640
explains why capitalism has lifted the broad masses

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.700
out of poverty.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.540
That's the message businesses should be emphasizing,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.710
he says, and it's inspiring enough.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:02.040
Mackey and Brooks went head to head at an event

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.837
hosted in front of a sold-out audience of 200 people

00:01:04.837 --> 00:01:07.620
at the Brownwood Hotel and Spa in Florida.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.940
John Mackey wasn't able to make it to the event

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.240
because of weather conditions in Austin, Texas,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.007
but he appeared via Zoom.

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:16.930
A clearly articulated purpose, consciously embodied

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.280
by the leadership, should be an essential element

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.550
in all business organizations, while also enhancing

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:23.630
shareholder value.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:26.260
John I'm going to interrupt you when you have

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:29.230
five minutes to go, one minute to go and 30 seconds

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:33.750
just to warn you, so take it away John, on that resolution.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:34.740
Go ahead.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:35.573
Thanks Gene.

00:01:35.573 --> 00:01:38.760
I'm going to bring my slides up now.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:39.593
Okay, great.

00:01:39.593 --> 00:01:42.330
So anyway, sorry, I can't be with you.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.060
I tried very hard to get there.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.890
Bad weather. What can I say?

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.920
So this debate resolution is a mouthful.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.520
It is, a clearly articulated purpose consciously invited

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.540
by the leadership should be an essential element

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.200
in all business organizations,

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.280
while also enhancing shareholder value.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.730
What the heck does that actually mean?

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:04.020
We couldn't quite get an agreement on the topic

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.060
and this was somewhat of a compromise I think,

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.400
but essentially the way I interpret it is

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.250
that, does business have a higher purpose

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:15.123
besides only maximizing profits?

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.940
And yeah, I think it does.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.840
And that's what I'm going to talk about.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.360
So let me declare my bias upfront.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.760
I am a fanatical capitalist.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.730
I believe capitalism is the most important

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.770
thing that humanity's collectively ever done.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.650
And I'm going to demonstrate that briefly

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:37.161
because people need to understand how

00:02:37.161 --> 00:02:39.810
incredible capitalism is.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:43.410
Starting with, we can see the increasing wealth that

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.520
capitalism has produced.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.840
For most of human history, humans have been incredibly poor.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.910
It wasn't until capitalism and the industrial revolution

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:57.363
that humanity began to escape its poverty conditions.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:01.920
If you go back, it's unprecedented, just 200 years ago

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.440
the world's economy was at the level

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.563
of Sierra Leone is today: 94 percent of everyone alive were poor.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.203
Now the per capita income has grown by over 10X,

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.630
over 24X in the United States, 41X in Hong Kong,

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.773
75X in South Korea.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.053
It's incredible. How about poverty?

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:30.180
Again 94% of everyone alive back in 1820 was living

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.710
on less than 2.00 dollars a day in today's dollar. 94 percent.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.603
85 percent lived on less than 1.00 dollar a day.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:44.150
Economic freedom has a lot to do with whether or not how,

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:48.670
economically prosperous any type of country is.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.910
The more economically free it is the higher

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:52.973
the GDP per capita.

00:03:56.805 --> 00:04:00.070
The poorest people, it's better to be poor in a

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:02.670
country like the United States, with economic freedom

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.240
than in another poorer country.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:08.393
So economic freedom also helps the poorest.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:14.133
Life expectancy has gone from about 30 years,

00:04:14.133 --> 00:04:19.133
200 years ago now, to 72.6 across the world

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:22.650
and usually close to 80 in more developed, economically

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:23.883
prosperous countries.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.640
Illiteracy, it's hard to believe, but 200 years ago

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:31.310
88 percent of every human being alive was illiterate.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:34.850
And that's dropped now down to about 12%.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:37.420
Here's the economic freedom index, I put this up there

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.100
to make a couple of points.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:41.550
The first one is is the United States

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:43.760
which would have been, has been number one,

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.580
number two, or number three for most of our history.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.850
It's fallen, we've fallen.

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.570
Economic freedom is in decline in the United States,

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.830
and as a result our prosperity, while still very good,

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:57.880
it's not growing at the same rate

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.007
that it once did.

